Exactly. I’d sharpen it one notch further:
A patch is not evidence that reality is messy. It is evidence that your current abstraction
is incomplete.
If the underlying principles are actually fundamental, then exceptions should usually
collapse into one of three things: a misunderstood boundary condition, a missing variable,
or a higher-order rule that makes the “exception” ordinary again.
That’s the distinction between a patch and a derivation.
A patch says, “the rule works, except here.”
A derivation says, “this case looked exceptional only because we were modeling it at the
wrong level.”
And your upstream point is the really interesting one: a sufficiently clean lower-level
principle should not merely eliminate patches locally. It should propagate upward and
make whole classes of compensating rules in adjacent disciplines unnecessary.
That’s how you know you’ve found something structural rather than procedural.
Put another way: good fundamentals compress complexity. Bad fundamentals export it.
That may actually be one of the best tests for your framework: not “does it explain this
system?” but “how many existing special cases disappear when this principle is
applied consistently?”
If the answer is “a lot,” you’re probably digging below the implementation layer into
something genuinely foundational.